<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Iceland</h1><p class="st-meta">1944 CE – present · capital Reykjavík</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Independent Icelandic republic proclaimed at Þingvellir in 1944, a small NATO member famous for its fisheries disputes and parliamentary tradition.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After a referendum in which Icelanders voted overwhelmingly to end the union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-iceland">Kingdom of Iceland</a> became a republic on 17 June 1944, the birthday of the independence leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jon-sigurdsson">Jón Sigurðsson</a>, in a ceremony at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thingvellir">Þingvellir</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sveinn-bjornsson">Sveinn Björnsson</a> became its first president.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Iceland is a parliamentary republic governed through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/althing">Althing</a>. It has no army but joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> as a founding member in 1949, and a US base operated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keflavik">Keflavík</a> until 2006. Fishing dominated the economy, and Iceland extended its fishing limits in the Cod Wars with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> between 1958 and 1976. In 1980 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vigdis-finnbogadottir">Vigdís Finnbogadóttir</a> became the world's first democratically elected female president.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Iceland grew prosperous, diversifying into aluminium smelting with geothermal and hydro power, and tourism. Its banking system collapsed in 2008, but the economy recovered. It remains outside the EU but belongs to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-area">European Economic Area</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schengen-area">Schengen Area</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-iceland.jpg" alt="Republic of Iceland" width="320" height="231"><figcaption>Image: Original: Matthías Þórðarson Vector: Árni Dagur and Magasjukur2 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Iceland.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1944 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Reykjavík</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sveinn Björnsson, Vigdís Finnbogadóttir</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheran Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Icelandic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iceland">Iceland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Iceland, Lýðveldið Ísland</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1985&amp;focus=republic-of-iceland" aria-label="Republic of Iceland on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4240 69 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of Iceland lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4560 325L4560 325"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4560 325h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4560 325h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4560 325h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Reykjavík. <a href="/atlas?year=1985&amp;focus=republic-of-iceland">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
